data.content:
Your code **doesn't use `Thread.Abort`**, so it won't throw `PlatformNotSupportedException` on .NET 8. However, it still uses raw `Thread` which is discouraged in modern .NET. Here's the recommended migration:

**Replace with `Parallel.ForEach` or `Task`-based approach:**

```csharp
public class SimpleWorker
{
    public void RunBatch(List<WorkItem> items)
    {
        Parallel.ForEach(items, item => Process(item));
    }

    // Or for cancellation support (replaces Thread.Abort patterns):
    public async Task RunBatchAsync(List<WorkItem> items, CancellationToken ct = default)
    {
        await Parallel.ForEachAsync(items, ct, async (item, token) =>
        {
            token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
            Process(item);
            await Task.CompletedTask;
        });
    }

    private void Process(WorkItem item)
    {
        Thread.Sleep(100);
        item.Result = ComputeResult(item.Input);
    }
}
```

**Key .NET 8 Thread.Abort migration rules:**

| .NET Framework pattern | .NET 8 replacement |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationToken` + cooperative cancellation |
| `ThreadAbortException` catch | `OperationCanceledException` catch |
| Raw `Thread` for parallelism | `Parallel.ForEach` / `Task.Run` |
| `Thread.Join()` with timeout | `Task.WhenAll` + `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter` |

Your code is safe to migrate as-is, but switching to `Parallel.ForEach` gives you thread pool efficiency and built-in cancellation support without needing manual thread management.
